WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.800
Speaker 1: Donald Trump knew that he lost the&nbsp;
2020 election to Joe Biden and admitted as much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:10.400
to a White House aide in November of 2020. This&nbsp;
actually could have implications having to do with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:15.600
whether he's going to get charged as a result of&nbsp;
the January 6th hearings or in that investigation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:20.240
that's going on currently in Georgia, because it&nbsp;
goes to the former president's intent, which is an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:26.000
important element if you're trying to show that&nbsp;
he was willingly deceiving the American people.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:30.560
The aide is Alissa Faith Griffin, who worked&nbsp;
as the White House director of strategic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:35.200
communications. She went on CNN's State of the&nbsp;
Union with Dana Bash and said the following.&nbsp;

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:39.600
Speaker 3: One of the questions that we've&nbsp;
been asking since these hearings started&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:44.800
is whether or not the committee can prove&nbsp;
intent, whether the president intentionally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:52.960
tried to overturn the election knowing that he&nbsp;
actually lost. He told you in November of 2020&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:56.400
that he lost when you were working in the&nbsp;
White House, he admitted, he blurted out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:00.720
watching Joe Biden on TV, Can you believe I&nbsp;
lost to this guy? And he actually admitted in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:05.280
a press conference when he was speaking to the&nbsp;
about coronavirus in the press briefing room,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:10.240
he actually flipped and kind of admitted that Joe&nbsp;
Biden won. I think intent is going to be hard.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:14.880
I'm not of the mind that this is going to take&nbsp;
down Donald Trump in a legal sort of way. But I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:19.520
do think it's going to inform the public about a&nbsp;
man who lost and couldn't do what we've done for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:24.320
the entirety of our history, which is, you know,&nbsp;
allow a peaceful transition of power and allow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:28.960
who the voters elected to step into to office.
Speaker 1: Can you believe I lost the election&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:33.600
to this guy is allegedly the quote and as&nbsp;
loss Faith Griffin and Dana Bash points out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:38.480
you have a much stronger case of criminal intent.&nbsp;
If Trump is privately accepting the fact that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:42.480
he lost the election fairly and squarely, while&nbsp;
at the same time going out in public and saying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:46.080
that it was rigged and it was stolen from him&nbsp;
and all of these things. But there's a couple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:52.160
of reasons why I don't think that this quote in&nbsp;
this account make it a slam dunk case by any means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:57.600
that Trump had any sort of criminal intent. For&nbsp;
one, you're relying on the word of a former aide,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:01.200
which, although I'm sure she's credible and&nbsp;
you could probably get her to cooperate with an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:05.520
investigation and testify and all these different&nbsp;
things. It's not on video. It's not like we have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:10.160
the audio recording of Donald Trump saying this.&nbsp;
And as far as we know, it's not corroborated by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:15.760
other sources. So that's one. She referenced him&nbsp;
admitting he lost in another instance during that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:19.920
coronavirus press briefing. But it could have been&nbsp;
one of those slip of the tongue type of things.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:24.000
Like sometimes he would give this half hearted&nbsp;
concession where he'd say something like a new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:28.560
administration will soon be taking over, which is&nbsp;
just sort of accepting the fact that someone else&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:33.120
is going to be taking over the role as president&nbsp;
without accepting that he just lost the election&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:37.760
fairly. So it's a little bit different there.&nbsp;
Number two, and I think this is more important.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:43.920
I'm of the belief that as the election results&nbsp;
were pouring in and Biden was declared the winner&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:48.560
a couple of days after the 2020 election.&nbsp;
Trump. Knew that he lost and he knew that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:53.600
he simply didn't get the votes that he needed to&nbsp;
win. I don't think that initially he was actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:58.800
a believer in the voter fraud stuff that he was&nbsp;
talking about. But then as the weeks and months&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:05.200
went on, my sense at least is that he actually did&nbsp;
start to believe it. We suspect, without being the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:10.000
ones to diagnose him or anything like that, that&nbsp;
Donald Trump has narcissistic personality disorder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:16.800
or something similar to it. So he was just unable&nbsp;
to reconcile the facts that he lost because he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:21.200
thinks so highly of himself. Right. It's this&nbsp;
cognitive dissonance that he couldn't get beyond.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:26.400
And so the way that he decided to deal with it&nbsp;
was slowly but surely convincing himself that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:32.240
actually won the election and surrounding himself&nbsp;
with people like Mike Lindell and Sidney Powell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:36.480
and Rudy Giuliani and Michael Flynn, who were&nbsp;
telling him that, oh, no, no, no, you won.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:42.880
And he was eventually willing to suspend reality,&nbsp;
suspend disbelief, and just put aside his better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:48.560
judgment and accept it, because that went into&nbsp;
the narrative that he was trying to arrive at at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:53.920
the entire time, which is that actually he did win&nbsp;
the election. That's my sense of it all. And if it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.920 --> 00:03:58.640
really is the case that Donald Trump operates in&nbsp;
that way, it's going to be hard to show criminal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.640 --> 00:04:03.280
intent, because ultimately he really did believe&nbsp;
the things that he was talking about. And that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:08.640
makes it all the more difficult. So I do believe&nbsp;
that what Elizabeth Griffin said is true. I also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:14.480
suspect that that was early on in the process.&nbsp;
And by now he's changed his mind and he really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:18.800
does think that he won. We're going to be back&nbsp;
with you after the break. Stay tuned, everyone.

